WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.640
THE SCALE OF CRAIGSLIST:
Did you know that Craigslist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:07.880
is the 79th most visited website in the entire&nbsp;
world? In fact, Craigslist beats out several heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:15.240
hitters including Target, Yelp, Shopify, and even&nbsp;
Snapchat. With 250 million monthly active users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:19.760
Craigslist isn’t that far behind Amazon&nbsp;
themselves who boast just over 300 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:25.040
monthly active users though it should be noted&nbsp;
that Amazon shoppers are likely way more trigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.680
happy. It’s not clear how many dollars worth of&nbsp;
transactions happen on Craigslist every year. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:37.040
with that much scale and market share, Craigslist&nbsp;
could easily be a multi deca billion company even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.760
if they just charged a modest fee of 3% per&nbsp;
transaction. If they charged something more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:49.040
substantial like Amazon of 8 to 45%, they&nbsp;
could probably be a $100 billion company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:55.480
But in reality, Craigslist is nowhere near those&nbsp;
figures. In fact, Craigslist is lucky to even pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:00.920
in $1 billion per year because they don’t make&nbsp;
a single cent on the vast majority of Craigslist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:06.400
transactions which is by design. Craig Newmark,&nbsp;
the founder of Craigslist, definitely wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:12.160
the venture to be viable and profitable, but he&nbsp;
was never looking to create a tech juggernaut.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:17.080
His primary goal was always to just connect&nbsp;
buyers and sellers with no BS or thrills for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:22.480
as cheap as possible. And given that north of a&nbsp;
billion dollars translates to nearly $20 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:27.400
in revenue for each of his 50 employees, Craig&nbsp;
has never tried to take monetization to the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:33.680
level. In fact, that’s why Craigslist looks like&nbsp;
it hasn’t been updated in decades. It’s because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:39.240
hasn’t been. Of course, Craig’s team addresses&nbsp;
any UX issues or bugs as soon as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:44.600
but in terms of playing the UI game and&nbsp;
maximizing user retention, they’re just not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:50.000
all that interested. As far as they’re concerned,&nbsp;
they’ve created a reliable platform that serves a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:56.400
purpose. If that interests you, great. If not, no&nbsp;
worries. As you can see, Craigslist is basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.720
the antithesis to everything big tech stands for&nbsp;
whether it’s infinite growth, maximum profits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:07.120
or unlimited funding. But while they may not&nbsp;
be the most lucrative website in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:19.462
they’re no doubt one of the most impactful. So&nbsp;
here’s the insanely humble rise of Craigslist.

00:02:19.462 --> 00:02:20.600
HUMBLE BEGINNINGS:
Taking a look back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:27.080
the story of Craigslist dates back 70 years to&nbsp;
December 6, 1952 to Morristown, New Jersey. Craig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:33.160
was born to a middle class Jewish family that&nbsp;
was pretty ordinary. His parents held jobs like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:38.840
being a bookkeeper, an insurance agent, and&nbsp;
a meat salesman. That was until Craig turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:44.800
13 and his father died of cancer at which point&nbsp;
everything became a lot harder. If this was a Dhar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:50.600
Mann video, this might’ve been some sort of insane&nbsp;
pivotal moment. The point at which Craig realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:55.640
that he had to fend for themselves and start a&nbsp;
business and work his way up. But, in reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:00.640
this was nothing more than a sad and depressing&nbsp;
time for Craig. He didn’t have much of a social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:06.760
life to begin with, and now without his father,&nbsp;
he was not only a nerd but a broke nerd with thick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:12.280
glasses held together with tape. I don’t think&nbsp;
being part of the school choir, the physics club,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:16.960
the debate club, and the honor society really&nbsp;
helped Craig’s case either. According to Craig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:22.040
himself, he was possibly nerd patient zero.&nbsp;
To help him with his social troubles and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:26.840
passing of his father, Craig was regularly sent to&nbsp;
have therapy sessions with his school counselor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:32.200
but his counselor eventually gave up on therapy&nbsp;
and just taught him chess instead. Over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:37.960
things got much better for Craig financially.&nbsp;
After graduating high school in 1971, Craig would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:42.920
attend Case Western Reserve University. There,&nbsp;
he earned a bachelors and masters in computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.400
science and entered the burgeoning industry&nbsp;
of tech. Craig would score a programming job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:51.880
at IBM straight out of college where he helped&nbsp;
enterprise customers like Bank of America and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:58.360
Sun Microsystems implement IBM solutions. This was&nbsp;
akin to landing a job a Google today. So, little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:03.400
to say, Craig never really had to worry about&nbsp;
broken glasses or really money ever again but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:09.440
other aspects of his life didn’t quite improve in&nbsp;
the same manner, specifically his dating life. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:14.560
it’s not like Craig didn’t try either. In fact,&nbsp;
Craig would join ballet and jazz dance classes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:19.760
to meet women but given that he wasn’t exactly in&nbsp;
the best shape of his life, he would end up at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:25.760
hospital with a hernia. Before you knew it, Craig&nbsp;
had been working at IBM for 17 years and he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:31.320
in his early 40s. At this point, it seemed like&nbsp;
Craig’s fate as a lost geek who spent his entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:37.200
career at a legacy tech company was basically&nbsp;
sealed but Craig wasn’t willing to accept that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:42.920
He felt that what he needed was a completely fresh&nbsp;
start in a new city with new surroundings. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:48.360
with a burst of energy, in 1993, Craig would quit&nbsp;
his job at IBM, get a new job at Charles Schwab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:54.320
and relocate to San Francisco. And just as Craig&nbsp;
hoped this would indeed turn into a major turning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.120
point in his life. Unlike Detroit where he used&nbsp;
to live, in San Francisco, computers were not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:04.720
the nerd thing to do but the cool thing to do.&nbsp;
Craig started socializing with a bunch of computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:09.760
geeks who hung out at cafes and discussed the&nbsp;
revolutionary future of tech. And it was during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:14.080
one of these sessions that one of his coworkers&nbsp;
from Schwab would introduce him to something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:27.308
called the World Wide Web, and this would&nbsp;
completely change the trajectory of Craig’s life.

00:05:27.308 --> 00:05:29.600
HUMBLE RISE:
Jeff Bezos was introduced to the internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:34.560
around the same time. He saw that the internet&nbsp;
was growing by hundreds of percent every year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:40.520
and saw massive opportunity. Craig on the other&nbsp;
hand saw something completely different. From his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:46.280
perspective, the internet was the perfect gateway&nbsp;
to network with other like minded individuals and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:51.960
finally build a social life, and that’s exactly&nbsp;
what he did. He started regularly emailing all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:56.680
his friends from work about tech events that they&nbsp;
could attend together. Oftentimes, his friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:01.680
would forward these emails to more friends who in&nbsp;
turn forwarded them to even more people. Pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.960
soon, Craig’s emails started reaching dozens of&nbsp;
people most of whom, he didn’t even directly know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:11.480
Seeing this interest, Craig came up with the&nbsp;
idea to create an email list that people could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:17.160
easily join to stay up to date with local tech&nbsp;
events. And that’s why it’s called Craigslist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:22.960
it started off as Craig’s personal email list.&nbsp;
Pretty soon, Cragislist would grow into a strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:27.240
local community of hundreds of people who&nbsp;
suggested that Craig turn the email list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:32.680
into a website. And with that, Craig would whip&nbsp;
together a simple site that let people post about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:39.120
local events and craigslist.org would go live&nbsp;
in 1996. To Craig’s surprise, people would start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:44.800
using the site for things other than social events&nbsp;
almost immediately. We’re talking job postings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:49.960
recruiting, discussions, and so much more which&nbsp;
brought in a lot more people. Despite this massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:55.680
influx of users, to Craig, Craiglists was still&nbsp;
very much just a social thing. It had made him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.400
somewhat of a legend within the Silicon Valley&nbsp;
community, and he loved it. All of a sudden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.840
he was being invited to the best parties around&nbsp;
allowing him to meet tech leaders that he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:10.480
never even dreamt of meeting. But by morning time,&nbsp;
Craig was back at his desk at Schwab as a hardcore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:16.320
Java engineer. It wasn’t till years later in 1999&nbsp;
that Craig actually started pursuing Craigslist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:21.760
full time. In classic Craig fashion though, it&nbsp;
was nothing flashy. He basically just rounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:26.840
up 9 engineers in his apartment coded away, and&nbsp;
it turned out that one of these engineers was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:32.640
a super star: Jim Buckmaster. Jim had a very&nbsp;
odd background as he had basically spent his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:37.840
entire adult life at college. He didn’t have a&nbsp;
bunch of degrees to show for it though. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:42.160
Jim was actually a medical student at the&nbsp;
University of Michigan but he had stopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:48.160
seriously pursuing that by 1986. However, he would&nbsp;
spend the next 10 years at college just taking one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:53.800
class per semester while doing odd jobs so that he&nbsp;
could keep all of his student perks. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:58.680
he would stumble his way into California at which&nbsp;
point he would post his resume on Craigslist in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:04.960
hopes of finding a job. And it just so happened&nbsp;
that Craig himself would hire him. Truly quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:11.080
an odd back story, but don’t let that fool&nbsp;
you. Jim was an absolute beast. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:16.080
he was the guy who took Craig’s hobby website and&nbsp;
turned it into a serious business. He created a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:20.220
robust search engine, added discussion forums,&nbsp;
developed flagging features, designed the home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:25.400
page, and most importantly led nationwide&nbsp;
expansion. Seeing this rising super star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:30.560
Craig would give Jim the reigns to the company&nbsp;
and promote him to CEO in November of 2000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:36.280
a position that he’s held eversince. Over the next&nbsp;
couple of years, Craigslist would grow a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:49.143
nationwide presence but there was still one&nbsp;
major issue: the company didn’t make any money.

00:08:49.143 --> 00:08:51.000
HUMBLE DOMINANCE:
In 2004, Craigslist would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:56.320
begin charging users of certain categories for the&nbsp;
first time. Craig very much wanted the website to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:02.080
stay a free community resource, so he they mainly&nbsp;
started monetizing users with profit incentive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:07.840
For example, users who were posting job openings&nbsp;
or apartment listings. They started charging $75&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:13.240
per job listing in San Francisco and $25 per job&nbsp;
listing in other leading American cities. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:18.760
also charged $10 per apartment listing in New York&nbsp;
City. Many users started fearing that this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:25.200
the beginning of the end for Craiglist: it’s slow&nbsp;
descent into in maximizing proifts. And honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:30.480
if that’s the direction that Craig took it, I&nbsp;
don’t think anybody could really blame him. He had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:36.160
created a massive marketplace connecting hundreds&nbsp;
of millions of people, so wanting to monetize such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:41.960
a community is only natural. Not to mention, he&nbsp;
had a bunch of pressure to do so as well. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:46.800
after Craigslist started charging for postings,&nbsp;
eBay decided to get involved. They would purchase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:52.040
a 25% stake in Craiglists from ex employees,&nbsp;
hoping that Craigslist was gonna double down on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:58.080
monetization. But, Craig never went down this&nbsp;
path which really pissed off eBay. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.360
eBay would actually end up suing Craigslist&nbsp;
for not doing right by their shareholders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:08.000
and not focusing on revenue and profit growth.&nbsp;
But after years in court, eBay would just give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:14.040
up and sell their stake back to Craigslist in&nbsp;
2015. And to this day, select job and apartment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:19.480
listings are the only way that Craigslist makes&nbsp;
any money. That’s why they don’t even cross the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:24.640
billion dollar mark in revenue despite having a&nbsp;
quarter billion monthly active users. That isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:29.520
to say that Craigslist isn’t lucrative though.&nbsp;
While they may have an insanely high top line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:34.840
they do have an insanely high bottom line given&nbsp;
that they run an extremely lean operation. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:42.040
only a few dozen employees, Craiglist is often&nbsp;
able to boast net margins of 70-80%. So not too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:46.520
surprising that Craig has crossed the billion&nbsp;
dollar mark over the years. And most of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:51.240
employees are quite well off as well. As such,&nbsp;
Craig has largely stepped back from his role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:56.320
at Craiglist and his mainly been focusing on&nbsp;
philanthropy for quite some time now. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:01.400
it’s not uncommon for him to donate more than a&nbsp;
$100 million in any given year. And most of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:05.280
donations don’t go to your classic save&nbsp;
the African kids or save the environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:10.360
type efforts either. Rather, his donations are&nbsp;
often much more nuanced. He likes supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:15.680
efforts that create a more fair and free internet,&nbsp;
less biased journalism, greater cyber security,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:20.960
and more support for veterans. For example, if&nbsp;
you’ve ever wondered what type of person makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:26.480
large donations to websites like Wikipedia,&nbsp;
well you’ve got your answer. But putting all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:32.120
that aside, I think what makes Craig truly&nbsp;
unique is that he’s still just regular old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:37.280
Craig. If you saw him walking down the street,&nbsp;
you would have no idea that he is the Craig from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:42.520
Craigslist. He was never really interested in&nbsp;
becoming super rich or super famous or anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:47.720
eccentric like that. Craigslist was just kind of&nbsp;
something that happened by accident and he ran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:53.680
with it as wholesomely as he could. And I think&nbsp;
this statement really captures Craig’s entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:59.400
mission. “I am committed to customer service for&nbsp;
the rest of my life... Death is my exit strategy.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:06.080
And that is the insanely humbling story of Craig&nbsp;
Newmark and Craigslist. Unlike Craig, big tech has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:10.960
decided to go in the complete opposite direction&nbsp;
and it turns out that they’re now running out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:16.240
people to convert onto their platforms. Check out&nbsp;
this video to learn more about big tech’s endgame.

